WEBVTT

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:21.720
<v Ira Glass>So a horse walks into a movie theater, gets his popcorn and a Diet Coke, and sits down in one of the few seats that are left. He realizes right away there's a cow sitting directly in front of him wearing this huge hat, totally inappropriate to wear indoors, one of the big, tall Stetson hats, completely blocking his view. So the horse taps the cow on the shoulder. Excuse me?
<v Ira Glass>有匹马走进电影院，买了爆米花和健怡可乐，坐进为数不多的空位里。他立刻意识到面前坐着一头奶牛，戴着一顶巨大的帽子，在室内戴这种帽子非常不合时宜，是一顶又大又高的牛仔帽，完全挡住了他的视线。于是，马拍了拍牛的肩膀。不好意思？

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:46.150
<v Ira Glass>Cow doesn't budge. No response at all. So again, the horse taps him on the shoulder. Excuse me? The cow is like ice. He's like a statue. So OK, one more time. He taps him on the shoulder. Hey, can you take off your hat? Cow turns around. And he's like, oh, my god, it's a talking horse.
<v Ira Glass>牛纹丝不动。没有任何反应。于是马又拍了拍他的肩膀。不好意思？牛像冰块一样。像座雕像。好吧，再来一次。他拍了拍他的肩膀。嘿，你能把帽子摘了吗？牛转过头。他说，天哪，竟然是只会说话的马。

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:58.360
<v Ira Glass>Not the greatest joke in the world. But what I love about that joke is that for once in the joke, the animals acknowledge that we should not be having animals talking in jokes. That's the actual punchline of the joke. That's the premise.
<v Ira Glass>这并不是世界上最好的笑话。但我喜欢这个笑话的原因是，在这个笑话里，动物们第一次意识到，我们不该在笑话里让动物说话。这才是笑话真正的笑点。这就是前提。

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:13.120
<v Ira Glass>We as human beings are so constantly blurring the line between animals and people in kids' stories, and movies, and the way we talk to our pets. We can't help ourselves. And we do it so much we even have jokes where the animals call us out on it.
<v Ira Glass>作为人类，我们在儿童故事、电影以及和宠物交谈的方式中，总是不断模糊动物与人之间的界限。我们情不自禁。我们做得太过火，甚至会有动物反过来提醒我们的笑话。

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:37.520
<v Ira Glass>And I bring this up because that's our show today. Today in our program, we have two stories of human beings not just trying to blur the line between people and animals but trying to erase that line completely. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us, people.
<v Ira Glass>我提起这个是因为这就是我们今天的节目。今天在节目中，我们有两个故事，讲述的人类不仅试图模糊人与动物之间的界限，而且试图彻底抹去那条线。这里是WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听，各位。

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:55.140
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Monkey in the Middle. So there's some places where animals almost never go, places that are designed by humans for humans. This act ends up in a place like that, but it starts about as far from there as you can get. Dana Chivvis explains.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，中间的猴子。有些地方动物几乎从不去，那是人类为人类设计的地方。这一幕发生在这样的地方，但开始时却远在天边。达娜·奇维斯解释道。

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:05.210
<v Dana Chivvis>Our story begins deep in the rainforests of Indonesia on an island called Sulawesi. A few years ago, the photographer David Slater traveled there from his home in England to photograph a troop of monkeys.
<v Dana Chivvis>我们的故事始于印度尼西亚苏拉威西岛深处的雨林中。几年前，摄影师大卫·斯莱特从英国的家中前往那里，去拍摄一群猴子。

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:16.310
<v David Slater>I'd gone a whole day just following them into the thick of the forest, through the tangles and the vines and the muddy undergrowth and the cobwebs and the poisonous snakes.
<v David Slater>我花了一整天时间跟着它们进入森林深处，穿过灌木丛、藤蔓、泥泞的灌木丛、蛛网和毒蛇。

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.000
<v Dana Chivvis>Oh, man.
<v Dana Chivvis>天哪。

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:42.840
<v David Slater>You name it, I had to go through it. And it was just so amazing just to be like a monkey. I was a monkey honestly. I had met monkeys before, like I said, all over the world. And I know a little bit about monkey etiquette and showing your teeth-- not to show your teeth, not to stare them in the face. Yeah, this is one of the secrets, actually, of being able to get good photography is making the animal relaxed.
<v David Slater>只要你能想到的，我都经历过。能像猴子一样生活真的很神奇。我真的成了一只猴子。正如我所说，我以前在世界各地见过猴子。我知道一点猴子的礼仪，比如露齿——不能露齿，不能盯着它们的脸看。是的，事实上，能拍出好照片的秘诀之一就是让动物放松。

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:57.420
<v Dana Chivvis>There was one shot David really wanted to get-- a full face portrait with a wide angle lens. But every time he got close to taking the shot, the monkeys would look away. So after two days of these near misses, he changed strategies.
<v Dana Chivvis>大卫真的很想拍一张照片——用广角镜头拍摄的全脸肖像。但每次他靠近拍照时，猴子都会转过头去。在经历了两次几乎成功却又错失机会后，他改变了策略。

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:21.450
<v Dana Chivvis>He put his camera on a tripod and threw down some cookies to try to entice the monkeys over. He was hoping they'd get curious about the camera and start pushing the button themselves. They were curious. They were not pushing the button. So David attached a cable with a button on the end of it to the camera. The monkeys liked that. They started playing with it, putting it in their mouths, fighting over it.
<v Dana Chivvis>他把相机放在三脚架上，扔了一些饼干试图引诱猴子过来。他希望它们对相机产生好奇，并开始自己按下按钮。它们确实好奇，但并没有按下按钮。于是大卫在相机上连了一根带按钮的线。猴子喜欢那个。它们开始玩弄它，放进嘴里，抢来抢去。

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:31.680
<v David Slater>And of course when it got pressed, they automatically looked into the lens. And the fact that it's going ch, ch, ch, ch, ch, it's monkey language.
<v David Slater>当然，当按钮被按下时，它们会自动看向镜头。而且那咔哒、咔哒的声音，那是猴子的语言。

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:43.420
<v Dana Chivvis>They fired off a few shots. David reviewed them. They weren't bad, a little out of focus. So he adjusted the settings and put the camera back. The monkeys were into the whole project by now.
<v Dana Chivvis>它们拍了几张照片。大卫检查了一下。还不错，就是稍微有点对焦模糊。所以他调整了设置，又把相机放回去。此时猴子们已经完全投入到整个项目中了。

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:58.920
<v Dana Chivvis>In fact, they were getting a little too excited. They almost knocked the tripod over. So David laid down on his stomach and stretched his arm out to steady the tripod with two fingers. A few other monkeys noticed their strange new friend lying full out on the floor and hopped onto his back.
<v Dana Chivvis>事实上，它们变得有点太兴奋了。它们差点把三脚架撞倒。所以大卫趴在地上，伸出手臂用两根手指稳住三脚架。其他几只猴子注意到这个奇怪的新朋友趴在地上一动不动，就跳到了他的背上。

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:28.020
<v David Slater>So I had monkeys on my back whilst I'm trying to keep my camera from being knocked, while some monkeys, or two or three monkeys, are trying to play with the button. And all of a sudden, I heard the camera going off. And I didn't dare. I knew that if I looked up at them, they would probably look away. So I let them click it, click it, click it. And I sort of very carefully looked up to see them pulling these amazing contorted faces, like howling mouths, and the smiling mouth, and more besides.
<v David Slater>所以我背上驮着猴子，同时努力不让相机被撞倒，而另外两三只猴子在试图玩按钮。突然，我听到相机开始咔咔作响。我不敢乱动。我知道如果我抬头看它们，它们很可能会转过头去。所以我让它们点按，一次又一次。我非常小心地抬头看了一眼，看到它们做了各种神奇夸张的表情，像嚎叫的嘴，微笑的嘴，还有其他的。

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:33.710
<v Dana Chivvis>The monkeys were taking selfies-- amazing, beautiful, weird selfies.
<v Dana Chivvis>猴子们在自拍——令人惊叹、美丽又古怪的自拍。

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:40.040
<v David Slater>I just wanted it to go on forever really.
<v David Slater>我真希望这一切能永远持续下去。

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:57.650
<v Dana Chivvis>When David finally stood up and checked their work, he saw that the shots weren't just regular, old, everyday selfies. They weren't that guy at the gym taking pictures of his muscles in the mirror. These were next level, transcendentally good. They somehow escape the bounds of selfie and enter the realm of art.
<v Dana Chivvis>当大卫终于站起来检查它们的作品时，他发现这些照片不仅仅是普通的、随意的日常自拍。它们不是那种在健身房对着镜子拍自己肌肉的家伙。这些是更高层次的、超越性的优秀作品。它们以某种方式逃脱了自拍的局限，进入了艺术的领域。

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:26.900
<v Dana Chivvis>There was one shot in particular that caught David's eye. The monkey's face is very close to the camera looking straight into the lens. It's hair perfectly coiffed. Eyes friendly, big and round and amber. All of which are nice details, and details are important. But the focus of this selfie, the main event, is the monkey's self-satisfied grin-- teeth that are too big for its mouth with gaps between them, a real grin, like he knows he's smiling for a picture.
<v Dana Chivvis>有一张照片特别吸引大卫的注意。猴子的脸非常靠近相机，直视镜头。毛发梳理得完美无瑕。眼睛友好、又大又圆、呈琥珀色。所有这些都是很好的细节，细节很重要。但这张自拍的焦点，重头戏，是猴子那种自鸣得意的咧嘴笑——牙齿大得塞不下嘴，牙齿之间还有缝隙，真正的咧嘴笑，就像他知道自己在对着镜头微笑一样。

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:40.830
<v David Slater>I just saw it on the front cover of National Geographic or something like that. And I just thought, I need to get this to my agents.
<v David Slater>我当时就在想，这张照片可以做《国家地理》之类的杂志封面。我当时就想，我得把它发给我的代理人。

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:53.010
<v Dana Chivvis>David and his agents put out a press release about the selfies. They thought they could grab people's attention with the photos and then use the opportunity to send a message about conservation. And also they wanted to sell the pictures, of course.
<v Dana Chivvis>大卫和他的代理人发布了关于这些自拍的新闻稿。他们认为可以通过这些照片抓住人们的眼球，然后利用这个机会传达关于保护动物的信息。当然，他们也想卖掉这些照片。

00:05:53.010 --> 00:06:29.220
<v Dana Chivvis>These monkeys, crested black macaques, are considered to be critically endangered, which is just one step before extinct in the wild. They've lost 80% of their population in the last 40 years because of humans, which is why David went to Indonesia in the first place. He wanted to take photographs that would inspire us humans into caring more about these little monkeys, to stop killing them. The selfies didn't end up on the cover of National Geographic, but the British tabloids were interested. The Daily Mail was the first newspaper to publish the story about the selfies. This was in 2011.
<v Dana Chivvis>这些黑冠猕猴被认为是极度濒危物种，也就是仅次于野外灭绝的程度。由于人类的原因，它们在过去40年里失去了80%的种群数量，这就是大卫最初去印度尼西亚的原因。他想拍一些照片，激发我们人类对这些小猴子的关心，停止猎杀它们。这些自拍最终没能登上《国家地理》的封面，但英国的小报却很感兴趣。《每日邮报》是第一家刊登关于这些自拍故事的报纸。那是在2011年。

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:46.920
<v David Slater>Within an hour, it was all over your country, Australia, Germany. I was getting emails everywhere. My inbox went crazy. People wanting to know the story, wanted to use the image. Literally, my phone was hot with calls.
<v David Slater>一小时内，整个国家、澳大利亚、德国到处都是。我到处收到邮件。我的收件箱快炸了。人们想知道故事，想使用这张照片。真的，我的电话因为不断的来电而发烫。

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:57.270
<v Dana Chivvis>Over the next few months, the monkey selfies sold pretty well. David was licensing them to publications and selling prints. And then one day, he goes online to research the monkeys.
<v Dana Chivvis>在接下来的几个月里，这些猴子自拍卖得相当不错。大卫把它们授权给出版物并出售照片。然后有一天，他上网搜索这些猴子。

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:14.580
<v David Slater>I started to just search crested black macaque one day in the Google. And there was Wikipedia, top of the list as always. I clicked on that, and there's my image. And I thought, where have they got this from? Have they been to Caters News Agency and paid a license fee for it?
<v David Slater>有一天我在谷歌上搜索黑冠猕猴。维基百科排在列表第一位，一如既往。我点击进去，看到了我的照片。我心想，他们是从哪弄来的？他们去过凯特斯新闻社并支付了版权费吗？

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.450
<v Dana Chivvis>They had not.
<v Dana Chivvis>他们没有。

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:32.460
<v David Slater>So I sent them a letter. Sent them a letter saying, I think you're using this without a license fee. Could you show me one, or would you pay? They wrote back, saying, this image we believe is public domain.
<v David Slater>所以我给他们写了一封信。信中说，我认为你们在没有支付版权费的情况下使用了这张照片。你们能出示授权书，或者支付费用吗？他们回信说，我们认为这张照片属于公有领域。

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:58.580
<v Dana Chivvis>In other words, anyone could use it without David's permission, for free. To David, that was just stealing. He makes a living from selling his pictures. So it was really helpful to have one that was such a hit. But now, anyone could download it from Wikipedia and hang it on their wall or print it in their publication. Wikipedia's opinion is that information on the internet should be free. And David soon learned that they had decided the selfie was in the public domain--
<v Dana Chivvis>换句话说，任何人都可以免费使用它，无需大卫的许可。对大卫来说，这就是偷窃。他靠卖照片为生。所以能拍到这样一张轰动一时的照片真的很有帮助。但现在，任何人都可以从维基百科下载它，挂在墙上或印在出版物中。维基百科的观点是互联网上的信息应该免费。大卫很快了解到，他们已经认定这张自拍属于公有领域——

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.690
<v David Slater>Because the monkey pressed the button.
<v David Slater>因为是猴子按下的按钮。

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:18.500
<v Dana Chivvis>So the monkey was technically the creator of the photo. But in Wikipedia's assessment, monkeys can't own copyrights. Hence, the photo was free and fair for all to use. It belonged to the internet. David was baffled. He thought--
<v Dana Chivvis>所以从技术上讲，猴子是这张照片的创作者。但在维基百科的评估中，猴子不能拥有版权。因此，这张照片是免费的，人人可用。它属于互联网。大卫感到困惑。他想——

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:25.100
<v David Slater>Well, surely I've got the copyright. I set it all up. I've got the intent. I had the creativity. All the monkey did is press the button.
<v David Slater>嗯，版权肯定在我手里。是我安排好了一切。我有意图。我有创造力。猴子所做的只是按下了按钮。

00:08:25.100 --> 00:09:01.320
<v Dana Chivvis>Wikipedia includes a note below the photo, which reads, quote, "This file is in the public domain because as the work of a non-human animal it has no human author in whom copyright is vested." This decision of Wikipedia's, to declare the photo public domain, that was the opening volley in the monkey selfie battle to come. It was the moment when mother nature was shoved aside, and human nature, with its overstuffed baggage of laws and opinions and domination, took a seat.
<v Dana Chivvis>维基百科在照片下方加了一段注释，上面写着：“此文件属于公有领域，因为它是非人类动物的作品，没有人类作者拥有版权。”维基百科宣布这张照片为公有领域的决定，是即将到来的“猴子自拍大战”的第一波攻势。那一刻，大自然被推到了一边，而人性——带着它那沉重的法律和观点包袱——占据了主导地位。

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:22.080
<v Dana Chivvis>David went on the offensive, assembled a team of lawyers, studied up on copyright law. He even looked into finding venture capitalists to fund a lawsuit against Wikipedia. Wikimedia-- that's the foundation that owns Wikipedia-- they just gave him the finger. This was at their big conference in London. It's called Wikimania. One of the founders, Jimmy Wales, was there.
<v Dana Chivvis>大卫开始反击，组建了律师团队，研究了版权法。他甚至考虑寻找风险投资人来资助起诉维基百科。维基媒体——也就是拥有维基百科的基金会——直接对他竖起了中指。这发生在他们在伦敦举行的大型会议上。会议叫“维基媒体国际大会”。创始人之一吉米·威尔士也在那里。

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:44.640
<v David Slater>I soon got to know, from the good Wikipedians out there, that Jimmy Wales and many of the delegates were mocking me by printing out my image on great big boards that were placed all over the conference facility. And Jimmy Wales and various other people were encouraging the delegates to take selfies with my selfie.
<v David Slater>我很快从那些优秀的维基人那里得知，吉米·威尔士和许多代表在嘲笑我，他们把我的照片打印在巨大的展板上，放在会议场所的各处。吉米·威尔士和其他人还鼓励代表们拿着我的自拍进行自拍。

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.220
<v Dana Chivvis>That's just mean.
<v Dana Chivvis>那太刻薄了。

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:50.660
<v David Slater>There's quite a few of these images around the place on the internet, but the one with Jimmy Wales is particularly odious.
<v David Slater>互联网上有很多这样的照片，但吉米·威尔士的那张特别令人反感。

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:54.330
<v Dana Chivvis>Wait. I want to look that up really fast here. Hang on.
<v Dana Chivvis>等等。我想快点查一下这个。等等。

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:54.680
<v David Slater>Yeah.
<v David Slater>是的。

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.100
<v Dana Chivvis>Jimmy Wales monkey selfie.
<v Dana Chivvis>吉米·威尔士猴子自拍。

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.190
<v David Slater>Jimmy Wales Wikimania.
<v David Slater>吉米·威尔士 维基媒体国际大会。

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:16.380
<v Dana Chivvis>Here we go. Oh, yeah. Look at that. If you type Jimmy Wales monkey selfie into the Google, you'll find a photo of an adult male human holding his phone up in one hand and the monkey selfie in the other right next to his face. And he's got his lips puckered up like a duck.
<v Dana Chivvis>就是这个。哦，是的。你看。如果你在谷歌输入“吉米·威尔士 猴子自拍”，你会发现一张照片，一个成年男性手里拿着手机，另一只手里拿着那张猴子自拍，就在他脸旁边。他噘着嘴，像只鸭子。

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:21.170
<v Dana Chivvis>He looks ridiculous. Now, is he-- is that face he's making-- is that--
<v Dana Chivvis>他看起来很滑稽。现在，他是——他做的那个表情——那是——

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:24.140
<v David Slater>Well, it's like a duck face. I don't know where that comes from.
<v David Slater>嗯，那是鸭子脸。我不知道那是从哪来的。

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:24.480
<v Dana Chivvis>I don't either.
<v Dana Chivvis>我也不知道。

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.510
<v David Slater>But a lot of people seem to do this duck lips.
<v David Slater>但很多人似乎都喜欢这种鸭子嘴。

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:32.520
<v Dana Chivvis>How did you feel when you saw that Jimmy Wales selfie with your monkey selfie?
<v Dana Chivvis>当你看到吉米·威尔士那张和你猴子自拍的合影时，你感觉如何？

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:40.420
<v David Slater>So angry.
<v David Slater>太生气了。

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:57.750
<v Dana Chivvis>David was fighting Goliath. And then one day, the battle took on epic new proportions. It was like King Kong walked onto the scene, and now David had to battle him too. The dispute with Wikimedia had generated some press in the UK. And one day, David's wife was online.
<v Dana Chivvis>大卫在与巨人作战。然后有一天，这场战斗展现出了新的史诗级规模。就像金刚闯入了现场，现在大卫还得跟他战斗。与维基媒体的纠纷在英国引起了一些媒体报道。有一天，大卫的妻子正在上网。

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.930
<v David Slater>There it was. Photographer being sued by a monkey.
<v David Slater>就在那里。摄影师被一只猴子起诉。

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.450
<v Dana Chivvis>You found out the monkey was suing you because your wife found it online.
<v Dana Chivvis>你发现那只猴子在起诉你，是因为你妻子在网上看到的？

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:14.820
<v David Slater>Yeah. Just as I found out Wikipedia was stealing it because I stumbled across it, I also stumbled across the fact that I was being sued by a monkey.
<v David Slater>是的。就像我因为偶然发现而得知维基百科在偷我的照片一样，我也偶然发现我正被一只猴子起诉。

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:24.570
<v Dana Chivvis>The monkey in the selfie photos was suing him for copyright infringement with the help of PETA, People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals. David called his lawyers and said--
<v Dana Chivvis>自拍照片中的那只猴子，在善待动物组织（PETA）的帮助下，正以侵犯版权为由起诉他。大卫给他的律师打电话说——

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:42.900
<v David Slater>Look. Have you seen what's happening now? What can we do? And they said, Dave, this is clearly an April Fools'. Somebody is having you on. They said, just sit back. Let's see what happens. But do not respond to it. And you can't-- you've got nothing to worry about until you get served papers. Let's see what happens.
<v David Slater>听着。你看到现在发生什么了吗？我们该怎么办？他们说，戴夫，这显然是个愚人节玩笑。有人在捉弄你。他们说，先坐着别动。看看会发生什么。但在收到传票之前，你不需要——你没什么可担心的。看看会发生什么。

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:43.200
<v Dana Chivvis>Right.
<v Dana Chivvis>好吧。

00:11:43.200 --> 00:12:03.600
<v David Slater>And sure enough, one afternoon I was at home. And there's a knock at the door. And a man in a black suit was standing in my porch way. I opened the door. He thrust a large A4-sized white envelope in my hand. And he says, I've been instructed to pass these to you. So I said, what are they?
<v David Slater>结果，一天下午我在家里。门铃响了。一个穿黑西装的男人站在我门廊上。我打开门。他塞给我一个大A4纸大小的白色信封。他说，我受命把这些交给你。我说，这是什么？

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:23.640
<v David Slater>And I opened it in front of him. And I took it out. And I said to him, do you know what this is? And he said, not really. I said, I'm being sued by a monkey. And I showed him. And he started laughing. And we had a little chat. Because he was just a local from a local firm down the road from me. And he went on his merry way.
<v David Slater>我在他面前打开它。我拿出来。我对他说，你知道这是什么吗？他说，不太清楚。我说，我被一只猴子起诉了。我给他看了。他开始大笑。我们聊了几句。因为他只是我家附近一家当地律师事务所的人。然后他就高高兴兴地走了。

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:48.840
<v Dana Chivvis>I've read the complaint. It's kind of weird. You flip through pages and pages of legalese about copyright law. And then at the end when you get to the exhibits, it's just a bunch of monkey selfies, grinning, serious, howling monkey selfies-- like a legal document put together by Curious George. The monkey, whose name is Naruto, was suing David through his next friends, People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals.
<v Dana Chivvis>我读了那份诉状。有点奇怪。你翻过一页又一页关于版权法的法律术语。然后在最后，当你看到证据时，只是一堆猴子自拍，咧嘴笑的、严肃的、嚎叫的猴子自拍——就像好奇的乔治整理出来的法律文件。那只名叫纳鲁托的猴子，正通过他的“近亲朋友”——善待动物组织起诉大卫。

00:12:48.840 --> 00:13:09.030
<v Dana Chivvis>The case is officially called Naruto, a Crested Macaque, by and through his Next Friends, People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals, Inc., v David John Slater, an Individual. In legal parlance, next friend is a status like guardian. When someone can't represent themselves in court, the next friend can step in for them.
<v Dana Chivvis>该案的正式名称是“纳鲁托，一只黑冠猕猴，通过其近亲朋友善待动物组织，诉大卫·约翰·斯莱特个人”。在法律术语中，“近亲朋友”是一种类似于监护人的身份。当某人在法庭上无法为自己辩护时，近亲朋友可以介入。

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:16.470
<v David Slater>That's just the same as if it was a baby or a disabled adult who couldn't speak or represent himself.
<v David Slater>那和婴儿或无法说话或表达自己的残疾成年人是一样的。

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:17.330
<v Dana Chivvis>I see.
<v Dana Chivvis>我明白了。

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:21.080
<v David Slater>That's not unusual in human law.
<v David Slater>在人类法律中这并不罕见。

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:58.970
<v Dana Chivvis>PETA has a reputation for pulling stunts to attract attention to the plight of animals. Once, they dumped horse manure on the sidewalk outside Gordon Ramsay's restaurant in London after a guest on his cooking show made a dish with horse meat. They made a comic book called Your Mommy Kills Animals and then handed it out to moms who were wearing fur coats at performances of the Nutcracker. They campaigned to change the word fish to sea kittens so that people would associate seafood with their adorable kitty cats and stop eating them-- the fish, I mean. But Jeff Kerr-- he's the general counsel to PETA-- he says this monkey selfie case is not one of those stunts.
<v Dana Chivvis>善待动物组织以制造噱头来吸引人们对动物处境的关注而闻名。有一次，他们在伦敦戈登·拉姆齐的餐厅外的人行道上倒了马粪，因为他烹饪节目的一位嘉宾做了一道马肉菜肴。他们制作了一本名为《你的妈妈杀害动物》的漫画书，然后在《胡桃夹子》的演出场地分发给穿着皮草大衣的妈妈们。他们发起运动，将“鱼”这个词改为“海猫”，这样人们就会把海鲜和可爱的猫咪联系起来，从而停止吃它们——我是指鱼。但杰夫·克尔——他是PETA的总法律顾问——他说这个猴子自拍案并不是那种噱头。

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:18.830
<v Jeff Kerr>We first saw the monkey selfie in approximately 2014 when the dispute between Mr. Slater and Wikimedia became international news. Our view was immediately that Naruto, who created the photograph, should own the copyright to it.
<v Jeff Kerr>我们第一次看到猴子自拍是在2014年左右，当时斯莱特先生和维基媒体之间的纠纷成为国际新闻。我们的观点立即是，创作了这张照片的纳鲁托应该拥有它的版权。

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:28.220
<v Dana Chivvis>Jeff saw the dispute as this photographer wants to sue Wikimedia, and Wikimedia is claiming the photo is in the public domain. But who's standing up for the monkey?
<v Dana Chivvis>杰夫眼中的这场纠纷是：这位摄影师想起诉维基媒体，而维基媒体声称照片属于公有领域。但是谁在为猴子挺身而出呢？

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:37.580
<v Jeff Kerr>That's what the law is. And our argument, very simply, is that that should apply equally to him, even though he simply happens not to have been born human.
<v Jeff Kerr>这就是法律的意义所在。我们的论点非常简单，那就是法律应该同等地适用于他，即使他恰好不是人类。

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:49.820
<v Dana Chivvis>But certainly the monkey didn't think like, now this is great. I've got a camera in my hands. I'm going to snap a couple frames and update my Bumble profile with whatever I get. The monkey's not thinking of it as, I'm taking a photograph here.
<v Dana Chivvis>但当然，猴子并没有想，“现在这太棒了。我手里有台相机。我要拍两张照片，然后用我拍的照片更新我的Bumble个人资料。”猴子并没有想，“我在这里拍照。”

00:14:49.820 --> 00:15:21.710
<v Jeff Kerr>But that's not required under the copyright law. All that's required is that-- the copyright law simply provides that the author, the creator of the image, is entitled to own it. It doesn't say that the author has to know that he or she is making a photograph. If somebody gives you their camera and asks you to take a photograph, and you arrange them and you take the photograph, under those circumstances, you technically would own the copyright.
<v Jeff Kerr>但版权法并不要求这一点。版权法只是规定，作者，即图像的创作者，有权拥有它。它并没有说作者必须知道他或她正在制作一张照片。如果有人把相机给你并请你拍照，你安排好他们并拍下了照片，在这种情况下，从技术上讲，你拥有版权。

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:34.810
<v Dana Chivvis>There are like 20 French tourists that I need to sue.
<v Dana Chivvis>我有大约20个法国游客需要起诉。

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:46.550
<v Ira Glass>PETA is an American organization. And the case was to be heard in federal court in San Francisco. David talked to his lawyers in England. And they told him he needed to hire an American attorney and fight this.
<v Ira Glass>善待动物组织是一个美国组织。案件将在旧金山的联邦法院审理。大卫和他在英国的律师谈过。他们告诉他需要雇佣一名美国律师来打这场官司。

00:15:46.550 --> 00:16:01.610
<v David Slater>I was advised by talking to these attorneys that I really did need to follow this through, because there would be a chance, an outside chance albeit, of it just going the monkey's way if I didn't challenge it.
<v David Slater>通过与这些律师交谈，我得到的建议是，我确实需要把这件事坚持下去，因为如果不反驳，如果我不挑战，哪怕机会微乎其微，它也可能会按猴子的方式发展。

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.290
<v Dana Chivvis>The monkey would win?
<v Dana Chivvis>猴子会赢？

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:18.150
<v David Slater>Yeah. Well, it was in the court in California. And everybody has the opinion that anything can happen there. I don't know how true that is. But I was being told I can't take that risk. But of course--
<v David Slater>是的。嗯，是在加州的法庭。每个人都认为那里什么事都可能发生。我不知道那有多真实。但我被告知我不能冒那个险。当然——

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.260
<v Dana Chivvis>Have you been to California?
<v Dana Chivvis>你去过加州吗？

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:21.770
<v David Slater>Well, no.
<v David Slater>嗯，没有。

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:26.940
<v Dana Chivvis>It's kind of true.
<v Dana Chivvis>那是真的。

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:29.920
<v Ira Glass>David hired Andrew Dhuey to represent him.
<v Ira Glass>大卫雇佣了安德鲁·杜伊作为他的代理人。

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:36.130
<v Dana Chivvis>Do you remember when you first heard about the case? Was it when-- did David call you? Is that how you first heard about it?
<v Dana Chivvis>你还记得第一次听到这个案子的时候吗？是——是大卫给你打的电话？你是这样听说的吗？

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:51.010
<v Andrew Dhuey>No, I read about it. And I don't remember where I read about it. But I got pretty excited when I saw that it was filed in San Francisco federal court because I'm right across the bay in Berkeley. So I thought, wow, I would be perfect for this case.
<v Andrew Dhuey>不，我是读到的。我不记得是在哪读到的了。但当我看到它在旧金山联邦法院提起诉讼时，我感到很兴奋，因为我就在湾对面的伯克利。所以我心想，哇，我太适合这个案子了。

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:58.900
<v Dana Chivvis>Andrew is an intellectual property lawyer. Works for himself. And when he read about the case, he reached out to David to ask if he could take it.
<v Dana Chivvis>安德鲁是一名知识产权律师。自由职业者。当他读到这个案子时，他联系了大卫，询问他是否可以承接。

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:01.940
<v Dana Chivvis>Why did you think you were perfect for the case?
<v Dana Chivvis>为什么你认为你适合这个案子？

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:17.530
<v Andrew Dhuey>Well, I think the case kind of lent itself to making monkey puns and things like that. And I just thought that would be-- I kind of like to do that. I like to joke around. I don't know if you've seen my LinkedIn profile. I'm in a Chippendales costume.
<v Andrew Dhuey>嗯，我认为这个案子本身就适合编猴子的双关语之类的东西。我就是觉得那会——我有点喜欢那样。我喜欢开玩笑。我不知道你有没有看过我的领英资料。我穿着奇彭代尔舞者的服装。

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:23.110
<v Dana Chivvis>For his LinkedIn picture, Andrew chose a shot of himself wearing eyeglasses, a bow tie, and nothing else.
<v Dana Chivvis>在领英头像上，安德鲁选择了一张他戴着眼镜、系着领结、什么也没穿的照片。

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:40.820
<v Andrew Dhuey>All these years-- I've been practicing for like 25 years, and I've never been able to joke around in anything I filed in court. So I was like, hey, this would actually be a case where I could do that.
<v Andrew Dhuey>这些年来——我已经执业大约25年了，我从来没能在法庭文件中开过玩笑。所以我当时心想，嘿，这会是一个我可以那样做的案子。

00:17:40.820 --> 00:18:00.270
<v Dana Chivvis>That said, he's a real intellectual property lawyer. He's defended the toy company Wham-O, won an important case against Panasonic. And for Andrew, the case was a one banana problem. He answered Naruto's complaint with a motion to dismiss the case. Monkeys, he said, do not have standing to sue in federal court.
<v Dana Chivvis>话虽如此，他是一位真正的知识产权律师。他曾为玩具公司Wham-O辩护，赢得了一场针对松下的重要官司。对安德鲁来说，这案子是“一个香蕉问题”。他用撤诉动议回应了纳鲁托的诉状。他说，猴子没有在联邦法院起诉的资格。

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:27.280
<v Dana Chivvis>His argument is based on a precedent set in 2004 in a case called Cetacean Community v George W. Bush, in which all the world's whales, porpoises, and dolphins sued the president. All the world's whales, porpoises, and dolphins argued that the military's use of underwater sonar was hurting them, changing their natural behaviors, like eating and mating. Basically, sonar is just an incredibly loud sound wave.
<v Dana Chivvis>他的论点基于2004年一个名为“鲸豚社区诉乔治·W·布什”的案件中所确立的先例，当时世界上所有的鲸鱼、海豚和鼠海豚起诉了总统。世界上所有的鲸鱼、海豚和鼠海豚辩称，军方在水下使用声纳伤害了它们，改变了它们的自然行为，如进食和交配。基本上，声纳只是极其响亮的声波。

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:52.490
<v Dana Chivvis>So cetaceans, that's a scientific word for a category of marine mammal-- cetaceans flee when they hear sonar, sometimes even beating themselves to get away. In that case, the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals decided that unless Congress explicitly states otherwise, animals do not have standing to sue in federal court. So that's the case Andrew pointed to in his defense.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以鲸豚类——这是海洋哺乳动物类别的科学词汇——听到声纳就会逃跑，有时甚至因为逃跑而自残。在那起案件中，第九巡回上诉法院裁定，除非国会明确规定，否则动物在联邦法院没有起诉资格。这就是安德鲁在辩护中指出的案例。

00:18:52.490 --> 00:19:14.270
<v Dana Chivvis>But PETA hired an outside attorney to represent the monkey Naruto in court, a guy named David Schwarz. This guy, Schwarz, he comes from a fancy law firm in Los Angeles known for intellectual property law. Most of his case was an intellectual property argument that Naruto was the legal owner of the copyright because he was the one who made the photo.
<v Dana Chivvis>但是善待动物组织雇佣了一名外部律师在法庭上为猴子纳鲁托辩护，一个叫大卫·施瓦茨的人。这个施瓦茨来自洛杉矶一家以知识产权法闻名的豪华律师事务所。他大部分的陈述是一个知识产权论点，即纳鲁托是版权的合法所有者，因为他是拍摄照片的人。

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:26.090
<v Dana Chivvis>But PETA also saw this case as an opportunity to nudge the law and set a precedent. They disagreed with the ruling in the cetacean case. They believe animals should have recourse in court.
<v Dana Chivvis>但善待动物组织也认为这是一个推动法律和设立先例的机会。他们不同意鲸豚案的裁决。他们认为动物应该有权在法庭上进行诉求。

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:35.420
<v Woman>Calling civil matter 15-4324, Naruto v David John Slater, et al. Counsel, please come forward and state your appearance.
<v Woman>传唤民事案件15-4324，纳鲁托诉大卫·约翰·斯莱特等。律师，请上前说明你们的代理关系。

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:44.360
<v Dana Chivvis>This is a recording from the hearing in the federal district court in San Francisco. The judge asked Schwarz if he has an example of an animal ever being granted a copyright.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是旧金山联邦地方法院听证会的录音。法官问施瓦茨是否有动物被授予版权的例子。

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:53.810
<v Judge>In the vast history of common law prior to the Copyright Act, was there-- do you have an example of where an animal was granted a copyright?
<v Judge>在版权法之前的漫长普通法历史中，是否有——你有没有动物被授予版权的例子？

00:19:53.810 --> 00:20:18.200
<v David Schwarz>I do not have an example where an animal was granted a copyright. We could make the counterargument as to how other humans were denied protection under that law due to the color of their skin. One could see the contradiction in a statutory interpretation where we see that problem in the antebellum days. But when you look at the cornerstones of--
<v David Schwarz>我没有动物被授予版权的例子。我们可以反驳，在那个法律下，其他人是如何因皮肤颜色而剥夺保护的。在内战前的日子里，当我们看到那个问题时，可以在法定解释中看到矛盾。但当你看看……的基石时。

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.690
<v Judge>That's quite a stretch, Mr. Schwarz.
<v Judge>这太牵强了，施瓦茨先生。

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:45.320
<v Dana Chivvis>Just to pause on this for a moment. Yes, Schwarz is comparing a monkey's lack of copyright to chattel slavery, which is offensive and seems weirdly blind to the racist history of comparing black people and monkeys. Even Schwarz seems uncomfortable with it. He's saying that denying a monkey the right to own property because of its species is the same as denying humans the right to own property because of the color of their skin.
<v Dana Chivvis>暂停一下。是的，施瓦茨正在把猴子缺乏版权与蓄奴制进行比较，这令人反感，而且对黑人和猴子进行比较的种族主义历史似乎表现得非常盲目。连施瓦茨本人似乎也感到不舒服。他是在说，基于物种剥夺猴子的财产所有权，就像基于肤色剥夺人类的财产所有权一样。

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.770
<v Judge>That's quite a stretch, Mr. Schwarz.
<v Judge>这太牵强了，施瓦茨先生。

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:47.440
<v David Schwarz>Pardon me?
<v David Schwarz>你说什么？

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.050
<v Judge>That's quite a stretch. But go ahead.
<v Judge>太牵强了。但请继续。

00:20:50.050 --> 00:21:10.700
<v David Schwarz>And I certainly wouldn't make it the centerpiece of any argument here today. But it is fair to say that before the adoption of the 14th Amendment, the concepts of property ownership in the area of patents foreclosed the ability of a slave to claim ownership to a patent.
<v David Schwarz>但我肯定不会把它作为今天任何论点的核心。但公平地说，在第14修正案通过之前，专利领域的财产所有权概念剥夺了奴隶申请专利所有权的能力。

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:43.930
<v Dana Chivvis>What's going on here is that PETA sees the monkey selfie case in the context of a larger end game. If they can get a court to agree that denying a monkey the right to own property is the same as denying a human the right to own property, that opens the door for the legal system to completely reconsider the legal status of animals. An animal would be declared an owner of property instead of just being property.
<v Dana Chivvis>这里发生的事情是，PETA在更大博弈的背景下看待猴子自拍案。如果他们能让法庭同意剥夺猴子的财产所有权与剥夺人类的财产所有权是一样的，那就为法律体系彻底重新考虑动物的法律地位打开了大门。动物将被宣布为财产所有者，而不是仅仅被视为财产。

00:21:43.930 --> 00:22:03.070
<v Dana Chivvis>This isn't the first time PETA has tried an argument like this in the courts. In 2011, they sued SeaWorld on behalf of five orcas at SeaWorld Orlando and SeaWorld San Diego. Orcas are huge, 20 to 30 feet long. And they weigh about six tons. They typically travel around 75 miles in a day.
<v Dana Chivvis>这不是善待动物组织第一次在法庭上尝试这种论点。2011年，他们代表奥兰多和圣地亚哥海洋世界的五只虎鲸起诉了海洋世界。虎鲸非常巨大，长20到30英尺，重约6吨。它们一天通常游行约75英里。

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:28.060
<v Dana Chivvis>PETA said that keeping these orcas at SeaWorld in small concrete tanks against their will to perform for human entertainment, that was slavery. PETA lost that case. But SeaWorld has agreed to end its captive orca program after the last of their 27 orcas dies.
<v Dana Chivvis>PETA表示，将这些虎鲸违背意愿关在海洋世界的小型混凝土水箱中为人类娱乐表演，这就是奴隶制。PETA输掉了那场官司。但海洋世界已同意在现存的27只虎鲸死后，结束其圈养虎鲸计划。

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:52.230
<v Dana Chivvis>To understand why PETA is pursuing cases like these, you have to consider two very different pictures about how the world works. In one of them, humans are at the top of the food chain-- either because God wanted it that way, or because we figured out how to use our opposable thumbs so well that we could hunt and gather, and till the soil, and build cities, and create copyright laws. We make the rules for everything else in this world.
<v Dana Chivvis>要理解为什么PETA要进行这样的诉讼，你必须考虑关于世界如何运作的两幅截然不同的图景。在其中一种中，人类处于食物链的顶端——要么是因为上帝想要这样，要么是因为我们太擅长使用我们的对生拇指，以至于我们可以狩猎采集、耕种土地、建造城市并制定版权法。我们为这个世界上的一切制定规则。

00:22:52.230 --> 00:23:06.330
<v Dana Chivvis>And the other picture-- and this is the one PETA has hanging on their wall-- humans are not at the top. Humans are just another mammal walking around on earth. And if we're going to give ourselves certain rights as humans, we should extend those rights to our fellow animals.
<v Dana Chivvis>而另一幅图景——也就是PETA挂在墙上的那一幅——人类不是处于顶端。人类只是地球上行走的另一种哺乳动物。如果我们作为人类赋予自己某些权利，我们应该将这些权利延伸到我们的动物同胞身上。

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:10.410
<v Dana Chivvis>You guys are saying humans are not superior to other animals.
<v Dana Chivvis>你们是在说人类并不优于其他动物。

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:27.330
<v Jeff Kerr>We're not. We are just another member of the animal kingdom. And it doesn't-- it's just prejudice and hubris that prevents us many times from seeing that.
<v Jeff Kerr>我们并不优越。我们只是动物界的另一个成员。通常是偏见和狂妄阻止我们看到这一点。

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:30.450
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Jeff Kerr again, the general counsel to PETA.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是杰夫·克尔，PETA的总法律顾问。

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:50.250
<v Jeff Kerr>That property status is something that we think is just an abomination. Like Naruto, these animals are feeling, sentient beings. They should be entitled to fundamental legal rights beyond simply food, water, veterinary care, and shelter.
<v Jeff Kerr>这种财产地位是我们认为的一种令人厌恶的事情。像纳鲁托一样，这些动物是有感知能力的生物。他们应该拥有除了食物、水、兽医护理和住所之外的根本法律权利。

00:23:50.250 --> 00:24:30.470
<v Dana Chivvis>This idea that animals should have recognized, fundamental legal rights is not unprecedented. Three years ago in Argentina, a judge granted a habeas corpus petition filed on behalf of an orangutan at a zoo in Buenos Aires. She was being held in a tiny, crappy cage. The judge decided the orangutan had basic rights as a non-human person, including the right to freedom. And in New York state, an attorney is fighting to get the courts to recognize that two captive chimps, Tommy and Kiko, are non-human persons deserving of legal rights, just like corporations get.
<v Dana Chivvis>这种认为动物应该拥有公认的、根本法律权利的想法并非没有先例。三年前，在阿根廷，一位法官批准了一项代表布宜诺斯艾利斯动物园一只红毛猩猩提交的人身保护令。她被关在一个破烂的小笼子里。法官裁定，这只红毛猩猩作为“非人类人员”拥有基本权利，包括自由权。在纽约州，一位律师正在努力争取法院承认两只被圈养的黑猩猩，汤米和基科，是应该享有法律权利的“非人类人员”，就像公司享有权利一样。

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:47.160
<v Dana Chivvis>The thing is-- David Slater, the photographer who's getting sued by the monkey, he agrees with PETA on this point. In fact, he used to defend PETA to his friends when they grumbled about the stunts. David even worked with them once on a campaign to protect wild hogs near his home in England.
<v Dana Chivvis>问题是——大卫·斯莱特，那个被猴子起诉的摄影师，他在这一点上同意PETA。事实上，当他的朋友抱怨这些噱头时，他过去常常为PETA辩护。大卫甚至曾与他们合作开展过一项保护他英国住所附近野猪的运动。

00:24:47.160 --> 00:25:03.920
<v David Slater>That's one of the greatest shames on them is that they've attacked somebody who is trying to achieve the same goal as they are. Saving animals from suffering is a very noble cause. But I think sometimes they get carried away with their power.
<v David Slater>他们最大的羞耻之一就是攻击了一个与他们有着相同目标的人。拯救动物免受痛苦是一项非常崇高的事业。但我认为有时他们被权力冲昏了头脑。

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:14.970
<v Dana Chivvis>Did the fact that this group that's sort of on your side was the one kind of attacking you or opposing you, did that make you stop ever and think, well, now, wait a minute? Do they have a point?
<v Dana Chivvis>这个站在你这边的组织攻击或反对你，这有没有让你停下来思考一下，嗯，等等。他们有道理吗？

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:45.390
<v David Slater>No. I've had to think very carefully about what my philosophy is. Because the book, my Wildlife Personalities book, that's part of the court case, I argue for animals having more fundamental rights, like dignity, and access to their ancestral forests, and access to food. So I am an animal rights advocate.
<v David Slater>没有。我不得不非常仔细地思考我的哲学是什么。因为那本书，我的《野生动物人格》书，那是法庭案件的一部分，我主张动物拥有更多的根本权利，如尊严，进入它们的祖先森林，以及获得食物。所以我是一名动物权利倡导者。

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:51.170
<v Dana Chivvis>Naruto and PETA lost that first round in court. Andrew Dhuey called David to tell him the news.
<v Dana Chivvis>纳鲁托和PETA输掉了法庭的第一回合。安德鲁·杜伊打电话告诉大卫这个消息。

00:25:51.170 --> 00:26:02.180
<v David Slater>We just took it very casually. We just said, yeah, of course. Of course that's going to get thrown out. Andrew Dhuey said this all along to me. There's no way that this is going to go anywhere.
<v David Slater>我们只是很随意地对待它。我们只是说，是的，当然。当然这会被驳回。安德鲁·杜伊一直对我这么说。这事不可能有任何进展。

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.180
<v Dana Chivvis>And then the monkey appealed.
<v Dana Chivvis>然后猴子上诉了。

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:12.980
<v David Schwarz>Good morning, Your Honors. And may it please the court, David Schwarz on behalf of the appellant.
<v David Schwarz>早上好，法官阁下。请允许法庭允许，我是代表上诉人的大卫·施瓦茨。

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:27.700
<v Dana Chivvis>The case went before three judges on the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals. That's the court that ruled against Trump's travel ban twice this year. These judges are not messing around. Schwarz made it 30 seconds into his oral argument before one of the judges interrupted him.
<v Dana Chivvis>案件提交到了第九巡回上诉法院的三位法官面前。那是今年两次裁定反对特朗普旅行禁令的法院。这些法官可不是好惹的。施瓦茨在口头辩论开始30秒后就被其中一名法官打断了。

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:35.660
<v Judge>Mr. Schwarz, before we get there, there's a preliminary question which I'd like you to address.
<v Judge>施瓦茨先生，在那之前，有一个初步问题我想请你解决。

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:56.010
<v Dana Chivvis>At this stage, the attorneys and the judges are going to debate case law. And the judges have a lot of questions for Schwarz-- the first being, how are you qualified to be Naruto's next friend? You don't know the monkey. And then a few minutes later, Judge Randy Smith interrupts Schwarz to grill him about injury. That's the legal term for harm or damage.
<v Dana Chivvis>在这个阶段，律师和法官们开始辩论判例法。法官们对施瓦茨有很多问题——首先是，你有什么资格成为纳鲁托的近亲朋友？你并不认识这只猴子。几分钟后，兰迪·史密斯法官打断施瓦茨，盘问他关于“伤害”的问题。这是伤害或损失的法律术语。

00:26:56.010 --> 00:27:20.330
<v Judge Randy Smith>Well, what's your injury? There's no way to acquire or hold some money which the copyright would give. There's no losses to reputation. There's no even allegation that the copyright could have benefited somehow Naruto. What financial benefits apply to him? There's nothing.
<v Judge Randy Smith>那么，你的伤害是什么？版权带来的钱，根本没法获得或持有。没有声誉损失。甚至没有指控说版权可能以某种方式使纳鲁托受益。他能获得什么经济利益？什么都没有。

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:26.500
<v David Schwarz>But, Your Honor, the redressability issue doesn't necessarily turn on the compensation. It could be--
<v David Schwarz>但是，法官阁下，可补救性问题并不一定取决于补偿。可能是——

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:36.140
<v Judge Randy Smith>Well, I want to know what then is the injury. There's no case that says copyright infringement itself is injury. So what injury do we have?
<v Judge Randy Smith>嗯，我想知道那伤害究竟是什么。没有案件说侵犯版权本身就是伤害。那么我们有什么伤害？

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:46.460
<v Dana Chivvis>Whatever the legal merits of this case are, there's just something really enjoyable about hearing lawyers throw around terms like redressability and compensation and injury when they're talking about a monkey.
<v Dana Chivvis>无论此案的法律依据是什么，听到律师在谈论猴子时使用“可补救性”、“补偿”和“伤害”等术语，确实有些非常有趣的地方。

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:56.710
<v Judge Randy Smith>I mean, if you give me a case that says copyright infringement itself is injury, I'll believe you. But I don't think you've given me one. What's your injury?
<v Judge Randy Smith>我是说，如果你能给我一个案例说侵犯版权本身就是伤害，我会相信你。但我不认为你给我提供过。你的伤害是什么？

00:27:56.710 --> 00:28:15.500
<v Dana Chivvis>The judges and Schwarz volley over case law and legal precedent and the definition of author for 20 minutes before handing the floor to David's lawyer, Andrew Dhuey. And after two years of research and legal filings and oral arguments, Dhuey finally gets his wish to make a monkey joke in court.
<v Dana Chivvis>法官和施瓦茨就判例法、法律先例和“作者”的定义进行了20分钟的激辩，然后将发言权交给了大卫的律师安德鲁·杜伊。经过两年的研究、法律文件提交和口头辩论，杜伊终于得偿所愿，在法庭上讲了一个猴子笑话。

00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:20.630
<v Andrew Dhuey>Monkey see, monkey sue will not do in federal court.
<v Andrew Dhuey>“猴子见，猴子诉”在联邦法院行不通。

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:43.670
<v Dana Chivvis>After that, his oral arguments can pretty much be summed up as, you should throw this case out. And can you please make PETA pay my legal fees? Once Dhuey is done, Angela Dunning gets up. She's the codefendant's attorney. PETA also sued the self-publishing book company that David had used. So Angela Dunning gets up to deliver her defense, which is also pretty straightforward.
<v Dana Chivvis>在那之后，他的口头辩论基本上可以总结为：你应该驳回这个案子。还有，能不能请你让PETA支付我的律师费？杜伊说完后，安吉拉·邓宁站了起来。她是共同被告的律师。PETA也起诉了大卫使用的一家自助出版图书公司。所以安吉拉·邓宁站起来进行辩护，也非常直接。

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:51.470
<v Angela Dunning>Naruto can't benefit financially from his work. He's a monkey.
<v Angela Dunning>纳鲁托无法从他的作品中获得经济利益。他是一只猴子。

00:28:51.470 --> 00:29:00.740
<v Dana Chivvis>David Slater wasn't in court in San Francisco that day. He didn't want to spend the money to travel all the way to California from Wales. But he watched the whole hearing online.
<v Dana Chivvis>大卫·斯莱特那天没有在旧金山的法庭。他不想花钱从威尔士一直飞到加州。但他在线观看了整个听证会。

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:15.320
<v David Slater>When I watched it-- and you see three judges there. And then one attorney comes up, and then my attorney comes up, Andrew and then Angela, and they started talking about monkeys. It made it real all of a sudden.
<v David Slater>当我观看时——你看到那里有三位法官。然后一位律师上来，然后是我的律师，安德鲁，还有安吉拉，他们开始谈论猴子。那一刻，这变得真实起来。

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:42.340
<v Dana Chivvis>That, yes, he was being sued by a monkey. Naruto didn't make it to the hearing either. He stayed in the rainforest in Indonesia. And to my knowledge, nobody asked him what he thought of all this. After two years, the humans managed to figure things out on their own. They told the appellate court they didn't want it to rule in the case after all.
<v Dana Chivvis>那就是，是的，他正被一只猴子起诉。纳鲁托也没有出席听证会。他待在印度尼西亚的雨林里。据我所知，没有人问过他对这一切有什么看法。两年后，人类设法自己解决了问题。他们告诉上诉法院，毕竟他们不想让案件判决。

00:29:42.340 --> 00:30:03.000
<v Dana Chivvis>And in September, David and PETA announced they'd reached a settlement. The details of the settlement are confidential. So I can't tell you much about it. But here's what I do know. David Slater still has the monkey selfie copyright registered in his name in two countries. And he agreed to donate 25% of the proceeds of the photos to protect the crested macaques.
<v Dana Chivvis>9月，大卫和PETA宣布达成和解。和解协议的细节是保密的。所以我不能多说。但我知道的是：大卫·斯莱特仍然拥有在两个国家以他的名字注册的猴子自拍版权。他同意将照片收益的25%捐赠给保护黑冠猕猴。

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:15.580
<v Dana Chivvis>About Wikipedia, they've still got the monkey selfie up there for anyone to download. They still claim it's in the public domain. David will have to sue them to get it taken down, which he says he plans to do.
<v Dana Chivvis>关于维基百科，他们仍然保留着那张猴子自拍供任何人下载。他们仍然声称它属于公有领域。大卫将不得不通过起诉他们来将其撤下，他说他计划这么做。

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:34.670
<v Dana Chivvis>There's just one other thing that should be mentioned about Naruto. There were a bunch of monkeys around when the selfies were being taken that day. And PETA consulted with the primatologist who works with those crested black macaques. She's the one who identified Naruto as the monkey in the famous selfie. But David says that's not right.
<v Dana Chivvis>关于纳鲁托还有一点应该提到。那天自拍时周围有一群猴子。PETA咨询了一位与那些黑冠猕猴一起工作的灵长类动物学家。正是她确定了纳鲁托就是那张著名自拍中的猴子。但大卫说那是不对的。

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:49.330
<v David Slater>Naruto is a male. I distinctly remember it being a female. I distinctly remember that pink rump which the female macaques have.
<v David Slater>纳鲁托是雄性。我清楚地记得那是只雌性。我清楚地记得雌性猕猴特有的那种粉红色臀部。

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:57.100
<v Dana Chivvis>Basically what you're saying is is that Naruto is making a fraudulent claim himself.
<v Dana Chivvis>你基本上是在说纳鲁托自己在进行欺诈性索赔。

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.020
<v David Slater>Yes. It's the wrong monkey in court.
<v David Slater>是的。法庭上的猴子弄错了。

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:04.600
<v Dana Chivvis>He's taking credit for an artistic work that was actually made by a female monkey.
<v Dana Chivvis>他正在为一个实际上由一只雌性猴子创作的艺术作品揽功。

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:10.240
<v David Slater>Yeah. And I think the feminists amongst us should start to protest.
<v David Slater>是的。我认为我们中间的女权主义者应该开始抗议了。

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:21.120
<v Dana Chivvis>I'm sure she's not the first lady monkey to have this kind of experience.
<v Dana Chivvis>我相信她不是第一个经历过这种事的雌性猴子。

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:39.560
<v Ira Glass>It's in the nature of both humans and monkeys to horse around in front of cameras. But it's a uniquely human characteristic to spend hundreds of thousands of dollars, years of our lives, to go to court over it.
<v Ira Glass>在相机前“猴里猴气”是人和猴子的天性。但花费数十万美元，浪费几年生命，为此闹上法庭，却是人类独有的特征。

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:56.400
<v Ira Glass>Dana Chivvis is one of the producers of our show. Coming up-- our first act was about a monkey horsing around. Next up, we have a horse monkeying around. That's in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio. When our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>达娜·奇维斯是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来——我们的第一幕是关于一只猴子耍宝。接下来，我们有一匹马在“猴里猴气”。那就在一分钟后。来自芝加哥公共广播电台。节目继续。

00:31:56.400 --> 00:32:09.760
<v Ira Glass>This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme in today's show, So a Monkey and a Horse Walk Into a Bar. Today we have stories of people trying to completely erase the line between animal and human.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的不同故事，今天的节目是《猴子和马走进酒吧》。今天我们有关于人们试图彻底抹去动物和人类之间界限的故事。

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:32.190
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at act two of our program. Act two, If Wishes Were Horses. So there was a thing that we were interested in for this week's show that's at the heart of this new short piece of fiction by Amy Bonnaffons. Here it is. This is an excerpt from a longer story read by two actors, Grace Gummer and Geraldine Hughes. Quick warning that this story acknowledges the existence of sex.
<v Ira Glass>我们到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，《如果愿望是马》。对于本周的节目，我们感兴趣的一件事是艾米·博纳丰这篇新的短篇小说中的核心内容。就是这样。这是由两位演员格蕾丝·古默和杰拉尔丁·休斯朗读的较长故事的节选。快速提醒一下，这个故事承认性的存在。

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:49.360
<v Woman 1>Every morning we meet in the kitchen and unsheathe our needles. My roommate Serena delicately peels down her underwear. I count to three, hold her hip to steady her trembling frame, then jab, thrusting the handle until the fluid disappears.
<v Woman 1>每天早上我们在厨房见面，拔出针头。我的室友塞雷娜细心地脱下内衣。我数到三，按住她的臀部以稳住她颤抖的身躯，然后扎进去，推进推杆直到药液消失。

00:32:49.360 --> 00:33:13.390
<v Woman 1>Then it's my turn. We're in this together, at least for now. The two needles look identical, although their contents are different. We have different goals. Serena wants to become a mother. I want to become a horse.
<v Woman 1>然后轮到我了。我们在一起，至少目前是这样。两根针看起来一模一样，但内容却不同。我们的目标不同。塞雷娜想成为母亲。我想成为一匹马。

00:33:13.390 --> 00:33:41.140
<v Woman 2>What does it mean to be a horse? First, it means not being a person-- no credit cards, no fad diets, no existential questions, no more boring meetings or family dinners, no political allegiances or disappointments, no responsibility to anyone but yourself. How do I become one? It's quite simple. State your desire in writing, and we'll take it from there.
<v Woman 2>成为一匹马意味着什么？首先，这意味着不再是人——没有信用卡，没有减肥时尚，没有存在主义问题，不再有无聊的会议或家庭晚餐，没有政治倾向或失望，除了对自己以外对任何人都没有责任。我该怎么变成马？很简单。以书面形式陈述你的愿望，剩下的我们来处理。

00:33:41.140 --> 00:34:00.350
<v Woman 2>Is it expensive? No. Why not? Because there's not yet sufficient demand for the procedure. All the more reason for you to try before it becomes pricey and exclusive. Right now, it costs less than a Pilates vacation in Tulum.
