00:47:48.240 --> 00:48:08.830
<v Woman 1>Serena, too, was changing. The pregnancy had rooted in her body, and she blossomed. The first sonogram showed not one but two fetuses in her belly. Every moment she wasn't teaching, she was at the computer researching the development of the strange creatures inside of her.
<v Woman 1>塞雷娜也在改变。怀孕已经在她身体里扎根，她绽放了。第一次超声波显示她的肚子里不是一个，而是两个胎儿。她不教书的每一刻，她都在电脑前研究她腹中那些奇怪生物的发展。

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:33.080
<v Woman 1>I, on the other hand, found myself unable to sit still. I'd sit down, get through one paragraph, then feel it kick through me, the wildness, the aimless rage. Then one evening, I got so frustrated with my work that I stood up and kicked a hole right through the kitchen cabinet.
<v Woman 1>另一方面，我发现自己无法安静坐下来。我会坐下，读完一段，然后感到它在我体内奔涌，那野性，那毫无目的的愤怒。然后一天晚上，我对自己的工作感到非常沮丧，以至于我站起来，一脚踢穿了橱柜。

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:56.360
<v Woman 1>Serena appeared in the doorway, pale, one hand on the swell of her belly. We stared at each other, gripped by the same mute question. How much longer could we go on like this sharing the same space? That night, lying in bed, I heard the unmistakable sound of muffled weeping.
<v Woman 1>塞雷娜出现在门口，脸色苍白，一只手按在隆起的腹部。我们盯着对方，被同一个沉默的问题所困扰。我们还能这样共享空间多久？那天晚上，躺在床上，我听到了无法辨认的压抑哭泣声。

00:48:56.360 --> 00:49:25.630
<v Woman 1>I got up, knocked lightly on Serena's door. I walked in, sat on the edge of the bed, lightly stroked her hair. "What's the matter?" I said. "Nothing," she choked out. But before I could respond, she corrected herself. "I'm terrified. This is the biggest thing I've ever done, and I'm doing it totally alone."
<v Woman 1>我起身，轻轻敲了敲塞雷娜的门。我走进去，坐在床边，轻轻抚摸她的头发。“怎么了？”我说。“没什么，”她哽咽道。但在我能回应之前，她纠正了自己。“我很害怕。这是我做过的最大的事，而我完全是一个人在做。”

00:49:25.630 --> 00:49:43.650
<v Woman 1>"You have your family," I said. "You have your friends." "Yeah, except for you." "You can come visit me," I said. "Bring your kids to the ranch." "Tell them what?"
<v Woman 1>“你有你的家人，”我说。“你有你的朋友。”“是的，除了你。”“你可以来找我，”我说。“带你的孩子去牧场。”“告诉他们什么？”

00:49:43.650 --> 00:50:00.730
<v Woman 1>"This is your aunt Cassie. She was restless and turned herself into an animal." "That's supposed to be an example of some sort? Things get hard, and you just leave? Just peace out of human life entirely?"
<v Woman 1>“这是你的卡西阿姨。她很不安，把自己变成了一只动物。”“那应该是一个例子吗？事情变难了，你就这样离开了？就这样完全退出人类生活？”

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:32.780
<v Woman 1>In the greenish light coming through the window, Serena seemed distinct and alien, like someone I had never seen, really seen, before. The protrusion of her belly was impossible to ignore. There were two whole people in there. For the first time, the terrifying marvel of this fact hit me full force. Perhaps her transformation was even stranger, even wilder than mine.
<v Woman 1>在透过窗户射进来的绿色光芒中，塞雷娜显得疏离且外星，像一个我从未真正看过的人。她腹部的突出是不可能忽视的。里面有两个完整的人。这是第一次，这一事实的恐怖奇迹完全击中了我。也许她的转变比我的更奇怪，更野性。

00:50:32.780 --> 00:50:58.000
<v Woman 2>Is the change reversible? No change is ever reversible. What happens if the process doesn't work on me? You'll get your money back. And we may ask you to participate in an ongoing scientific study of long-term centaurite health outcomes, for compensation. But you may decline.
<v Woman 2>这种改变是可逆的吗？没有改变是可逆的。如果这个过程对我不起作用会怎样？你会得到退款。我们可能会要求你参与一项关于长期半人马健康的科学研究，作为报酬。但你可以拒绝。

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:18.950
<v Woman 2>That's it? It's not possible for us to do more. Our hope is that the centaurites living among us will be viewed not as freaks or as failures, but as courageous-- female animals who gathered up all the uncertainties of their existence into one single, massive risk.
<v Woman 2>就这样吗？我们不可能做更多了。我们的希望是，生活在我们中间的半人马将被视为勇敢——聚集了她们存在的所有不确定性，承担了一次单一的、巨大的风险的女性动物，而不是被视为怪胎或失败者。

00:51:18.950 --> 00:51:38.880
<v Woman 1>I went for a checkup with the Atalanta doctor. The visit was routine, had been scheduled for months. But I was nervous. My progress since the last visit seemed to have stalled. She examined me all over and said "hmm" a lot. I grew increasingly worried.
<v Woman 1>我和亚特兰大的医生进行了例行检查。访问是例行的，已经安排了几个月了。但我很紧张。我上次访问后的进展似乎陷入停滞。她仔细检查了我全身，说了好多次“嗯”。我越来越担心。

00:51:38.880 --> 00:52:05.340
<v Woman 1>When I got home from the doctors, I saw that yet another delivery of baby stuff-- hand-me-downs from friends, large Amazon boxes full of equipment-- had arrived at the apartment and completely taken over the living room. To reach the couch, I had to pick my way over and between the boxes, stepping as delicately as possible with my horse legs, legs that were not made to do anything delicately.
<v Woman 1>当我从医生那里回到家时，我看到又有另一批婴儿用品——朋友给的旧货，装满设备的亚马逊大箱子——到达了公寓，完全占领了客厅。为了到达沙发，我不得不小心翼翼地绕过箱子，用我的马腿尽可能细致地踏过，那些腿根本不是为了细致做事而造的。

00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:29.530
<v Woman 1>Even when I got there, I couldn't sit down. It was piled high with baby clothes. I lost it. I whirled around and began kicking with an aimless violence that startled me with its force. I had not intended this. I was beyond intention. Like if I stopped kicking and hurling things, I would cease to exist.
<v Woman 1>即使我到了那里，我也无法坐下。上面堆满了婴儿衣服。我崩溃了。我转过身，开始以一种毫无目的的暴力踢东西，其力量让我感到惊讶。我没打算这样。我已经失去了控制。就像如果我停止踢东西和扔东西，我就会停止存在。

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:54.670
<v Woman 1>By the time I managed to stop, I had wrecked not only most of the new baby equipment but also the large flat screen television and the coffee table. I looked around at the torn baby blankets strewn with broken glass, the mutilated breast pump, the mangled stroller. Just then, a key turned in the lock, and Serena stood in the doorway.
<v Woman 1>当我设法停下来时，我不仅破坏了大部分新的婴儿设备，还破坏了那台大平板电视和咖啡桌。我看着撕碎的、散落着玻璃碎片的婴儿毯，被摧毁的吸奶器，破损的婴儿车。就在这时，锁里钥匙响了，塞雷娜站在门口。

00:52:54.670 --> 00:53:22.420
<v Woman 1>She looked from me to the mess, from the mess back to me. Her face hardened. I could offer no defense or consolation. I had only one option. "I'm going to leave now," I said. "I'll find someplace else to stay." She nodded with no expression.
<v Woman 1>她看着我，看着那一团糟，又从那一团糟看着我。她的脸僵硬了。我无法提供任何辩护或安慰。我只有一个选择。“我现在要走了，”我说。“我会找别的地方住。”她没有任何表情地点了点头。

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:44.890
<v Woman 1>I walked aimlessly, vaguely in the direction of the downtown hotels. My muscles grew leaden with shame. I felt like I was walking underwater. What pained me was not the notion that my wildness, my horseyness, had finally overtaken me.
<v Woman 1>我毫无目的地走着，大致朝市中心酒店的方向。我的肌肉因羞耻而沉重。我觉得自己像在水下行走。让我痛苦的不是我的野性、我的马性终于战胜了我的想法。

00:53:44.890 --> 00:54:24.070
<v Woman 1>It was the suspicion that my violence had been entirely human. That night, under a scratchy hotel blanket, I contemplated my situation. For the first time in nearly a year, I cried-- sobbing into the lumpy pillow, mourning the grotesque monster that I was, howling at my failure, my loneliness, my inadequacy as woman and as animal. Eventually, from sheer exhaustion, I slept.
<v Woman 1>而是我怀疑我的暴力完全是人类的。那天晚上，在粗糙的酒店毯子下，我思考着我的处境。这是近一年来我第一次哭泣——对着凸凹不平的枕头啜泣，哀悼我这个怪异的怪物，哀嚎我的失败、我的孤独、我作为女人和动物的不称职。最后，由于精疲力竭，我睡着了。

00:54:24.070 --> 00:54:45.200
<v Woman 1>When I woke in the morning, I saw that during my few hours of sleep the fur had finally reached upwards. My breasts were gone, replaced by a fine equine torso. I raised my hands to touch it, then realized that they had been replaced by another set of hooves. I felt practically ambushed by relief.
<v Woman 1>早上醒来时，我看到在睡觉的几个小时里，皮毛终于向上延伸了。我的乳房不见了，被一个精细的马匹躯干所取代。我抬起手去摸它，然后意识到它们已经被另一套马蹄所取代。我几乎被解脱感所淹没。

00:54:45.200 --> 00:54:59.020
<v Woman 1>I went downstairs to the concierge, asked her to use her human fingers to make a phone call. Red-faced and excited, she rang Atalanta. They would send someone that afternoon, they said, to pick me up.
<v Woman 1>我下楼去礼宾部，请她用她人类的手指打了个电话。满脸通红且兴奋，她拨打了亚特兰大。他们说那天下午会派人来接我。

00:54:59.020 --> 00:55:27.750
<v Woman 2>Can my loved ones visit me at the ranch? Yes. Will they recognize me? Most of them claim to, but it's impossible to determine how much this recognition depends upon wishful thinking. Can they ride me? We don't recommend it. So far, every attempt has ended in tragedy.
<v Woman 2>我爱的人可以在牧场看我吗？可以。他们会认出我吗？他们大多数人都声称会，但无法确定这种认出在多大程度上取决于一厢情愿。他们可以骑我吗？我们不建议这样做。到目前为止，每一次尝试都以悲剧告终。

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:54.460
<v Woman 1>I have four horse legs, and a horse torso, and a horse head. Outwardly, at least, I am all animal. I believe I still have a human brain mostly. But every day, its language grows rougher around the edges. For minutes at a time when I'm running or eating in the pasture, I have no thoughts.
<v Woman 1>我有四条马腿，一个马躯干和一个马头。至少在外表上，我全是动物。我相信我仍然拥有基本的人类大脑。但每一天，它的语言在边缘变得越来越粗糙。当我奔跑或在牧场吃草时，我有几分钟的时间没有思想。

00:55:54.460 --> 00:56:23.970
<v Woman 1>My brain is not empty exactly. It's as though a hot wind blows certain textures through my mind. My rage has diminished, but I am neither contained nor calm. I feel many emotions now, but they don't quite fit the words I know. I would describe them mostly as variations of active receptivity, of alert acceptance.
<v Woman 1>我的大脑并不完全是空的。就像一阵热风把某些纹理吹进我的脑海里。我的愤怒减少了，但我既不克制也不冷静。我现在感觉到了许多情绪，但它们并不完全适合我知道的词汇。我主要把它们描述为主动接受、警觉接受的变体。

00:56:23.970 --> 00:56:54.020
<v Woman 1>Somewhere soon, Serena will be teaching her children the words for things. This is a table. This is a chair. This is a horse. Meanwhile, my language is slowly departing, the words replaced by syllable and breath-- yes, mm, yes, huh, no, mm, [WHINNYING].
<v Woman 1>不久之后，塞雷娜会教她的孩子事物的名称。这是桌子。这是椅子。这是马。与此同时，我的语言在慢慢离我而去，词汇被音节和呼吸所取代——是的，嗯，是的，呵，不，嗯，[马嘶鸣]。

00:56:54.020 --> 00:57:33.010
<v Woman 1>Maybe she'll come visit me one day. Perhaps in my absence her hard judgments of me will soften, turn into questions. They will lead her to me across the long grass. And I'll look at her and nod. She won't try to ride me, but I'll let her approach, put a hand on my forehead. We'll both feel a sudden snare of recognition-- wildness face-to-face with itself.
<v Woman 1>也许有一天她会来看我。也许在我缺席时，她对我严厉的评判会软化，变成疑问。它们会引领她穿过长草来到我身边。我会看着她点点头。她不会试图骑我，但我会让她靠近，把手放在我的额头上。我们都会感受到一阵突然的认同感——野性面对面地看着自己。

00:57:33.010 --> 00:58:01.280
<v Ira Glass>Grace Gummer and Geraldine Hughes reading a short story by Amy Bonnaffons. It's a part of her collection of short stories called The Wrong Heaven, which is available for preorder at Amazon and elsewhere.
